Hi {!Case.Contact},

I noticed that there were a couple of leads that were returned for the reason "Customer did not request a quote". Please note that in order for you to have been sent a lead, somebody had to have come onto one of our websites (www.everquote.com or usautoinsurancenow.com most likely) and had to have provided us with all 42 pieces of information that we ask for. At the end of this process, they click on a button that says  "get a quote", which generates the lead.

In my experience, it is very unlikely that the person has "not requested a quote", since 100% of the consumers that sent over to you as leads have to come through our sites and go through this process to generate a lead.

I understand that other companies use different methods of obtaining consumer information, which may or may not involve the consumer directly requesting a quote, but our user experience guarantees that our consumers have expressed interest in receiving a quote as soon as they submit their information.

Sometimes when a consumer says "I didn't request a quote", they are actually saying "I don't want to speak on the phone right now with a telemarketer." You and I know that you are not a telemarketer, but consumers don't always understand this. We encourage the use of rating questions to get over this initial objection that some people may have to speaking on the phone.

For example, you can lead off by saying, "Hi John, I just put together a quote for your Toyota 4Runner. I'd like to verify your license number to make sure my numbers are accurate so that I can ensure that I save you the most money possible."

In that scenario, instead of leading in asking whether or not the person requested a quote, you assume that they have requested a quote (since we know that 100% of our leads were generated by intentful consumers), and you reassure the consumer that you already have their information and are looking to help them out.

I've spoken with a number of agents who have started using these tactics to drive through or completely jump over any initial objections, and since then, they have experienced higher quoting rates and in turn, higher binding rates.

I hope that you find this information useful in converting some of these leads into policy-holders. Please let me know if you have any questions about any of this.

Best Regards,